Update: 
10 July 2026: All zipped files are re-uploaded; I test them, and they are fine to be extracted.
The codes are also re-uploaded for the latest version and remove the "hard-coded" links.
I use another laptop (MacBook Air M1) for testing and successfully run the four main figures and reproduce the results 

Here are the instructions to perform all analyses and construct all figures for "Current policies and historical trends constrain future mass loss from the Greenland Ice Sheet" by Siew et al. 2026
Let me know if there is any problem (peter.siew@vuw.ac.nz)

Step 1: 
# Unzip all the files and put the unzipped folders in the folder that contains the "codes" folder
# Keep the existing file and directory format

Step 2: 
# You need the following Python packages
numpy, scipy, sklearn, xarray, matplotlib, statsmodels, ipdb, catropy, cftime

Step 3: 
# To run the main figures, you need to have the following in the folder "codes"
fig1_figS1_take_one_out_validation_autoregress.py --> Figure 1
fig2_fig3_GMT_SLR_fractional_idealized.py --> Figures 2 & Figure 3
fig4_new_damped_stable_overshoot.py --> Figure 4
tools_greenland.py --> supporting script with some functions
functions_read_gmt_training_data_seperate.py --> supporting script with some functions

Step 4:
# These are the codes for supplementary figures
plot_boxplots_CMIP5_and_6_ISMIP6_versus_emulations.py --> Figure S6
plot_posteria_model_multiple_histogram.py --> Figure S13
plot_sle_timeseries_PISM_four_simulations_ISMIP6.py -->
realistic_CO2eq_versus_CATS.py --> Figure S5

Step 5:
# These are the codes to create the necessary files (You don't need to run them if you can unzip all files):
create_GMT_global_and_Greenland_from_spatial_BEST_ERA5.py --> Create GMT for BEST
create_GMT_global_from_timeseries.py --> Create GMT for CMIP5 and CMIP6
create_damped_stable_overshoot_GMT_from_2100to2300.py --> Create the damped trends, stabilized, overshoot GMTs
create_obs_constrain_weight.py --> Create the parameter weights by history calibration
create_save_parameters.py --> Define the parameter range and create the 1,000 member by Latin hypercube sampling.
create_CATs_Nov2025_new.py --> Extract GMT from the xls file provided by Climate Action Tracker (Climate Analytics)








